  "structure": [
    {"description":"Zillow tables are wide: one geography per row and one month per date-named column. Filename prefixes identify city/state granularity, while the remaining filename identifies metric and housing segment.","objects":["zillow_city","zillow_state"]},
    {"description":"City_MedianRentalPrice uses monthly YYYY-MM columns primarily through 2019-12; ZHVI files use month-end YYYY-MM-DD columns spanning a longer period. Other Zillow series have their own coverage and missingness.","objects":["zillow_city","zillow_state"]},
    {"description":"acs_counties can align with other state/county data by geography, but identifier/name formats differ by vintage. State-level tables can align by normalized state and time at annual or monthly grain.","objects":["acs_counties","state_education","state_unemployment","zillow_state"]},
    {"description":"The city_crime partitions are mutually organized by population band rather than time; rates are stored as strings that may contain thousands separators.","objects":["city_crime"]}
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Blank cells are common in the wide Zillow time series and do not imply zero.",
    "Zillow files mix multiple header conventions and date ranges; inspect the chosen file's header rather than assuming identical months.",
    "Numeric population, labor, and crime fields may contain commas or trailing spaces and require numeric parsing.",
    "Geographic names are not fully standardized across ACS, Zillow, education, labor, and crime sources."
  ],
  "annotation_notes": [
    "Monthly Zillow columns are grouped as regular high-dimensional time families; all bounded identifier columns are listed separately.",
